  The Authority's letter came in October, folded inside a pay envelope, which was how Ruth Anker knew it was bad news
  before she opened it. Pay came by the supply boat every six weeks, in a brown envelope with the crown printed on the
  flap, and nobody at the Service had ever bothered to put anything else in one. A letter tucked inside was an event.

  She read it at the kitchen table with the kettle going cold beside her. The language was the usual careful language of
  people who had never stood a watch: [3min recognition of advances in automated navigation aids[0m, [3mthe Station at Skerra [0m
  [3mHolm will be decommissioned as a staffed facility effective the first of March[0m, [3mwe are grateful for your twenty-two [0m
  [3myears of dedicated service[0m. At the bottom, a signature she didn't recognize, and a line in smaller type saying a
  relief keeper would arrive on the next boat to handle the handover.

  She folded the letter back along its creases and put it under the sugar bowl, where it would stay until she decided
  what to do with it. Then she made the tea, because the kettle had gone cold and there was no reason to waste it.

  Outside, the sea was the color of wet slate, and the light was turning. She could see it through the window above the
  sink, the beam sweeping its slow circle over the water, white and then gone and then white again. Eleven seconds






